A||||8||CUTTER||Overheating Only When Sitting!!!||||5||
Z||000000||CUTTER||04-20-2000||01:20 AM||||My car overheats only when im sitting in one spot with the car running. It takes 5 minutes sometimes 10-15, It is fine when driving. I think it might have to do something with my heater/AC/defrosters not working. I dont think the fan works because when I put it on VENT and put the heat all the way up it blows hot air only when driving. If I put it on heat or ac/defrosters it doesnt blow any air at all. Could this all be one problem, or are they seperate problems? And how much will is cost to get this fixed?||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||04-20-2000||01:26 AM||||After your car warms up, just pop the hood and see if the fan is running.  That makes sense because you've got air flowing though the engine bay when the car is moving, but when you stop.... you have very little air flow.||||reg||1
Z||000002||CUTTER||04-20-2000||04:03 AM||||I dont think the fan is running, I know of a few junkyards that have fieros. Is it easy to install another fan in a 87GT? and do all years of fiero fans fit in 87GT's?||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||04-20-2000||09:25 AM||||To the best of my knowledge there are only two types of radiator fans for Fieros: single-speed and dual-speed.  All non-A/C cars had the single-speed; all A/C cars had the dual-speed.<P>It probably is your fan, or a bad connection to the fan.  My 86 SE had the same problem you describe, except that I could turn my fan on by turning on the A/C.  The guy I sold the car to said it turned out to be a relay.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Linx||04-20-2000||09:45 AM||||Ive never noticed on my 2m6, but on my van, (another fine GM product); when I put the computer into diagnostic mode (paperclip in the ecm connector), the fan kicks on.  It's an ecm, fan motor and relay test.||||reg||3
Z||000005||theogre||04-20-2000||11:37 AM||||Chad, Only some early 4 cyl setups had the 2 speed fan. All V6 and Most 4's, even with air, use the single speed fan.<P>Check the fan controls before replacing the fan. The fan itself doesn't fail very often. Find the fan sensor and ground the terminal(s) the fan should come on. If it does the sensor is bad. If it doesn't replace the relay(s) and/or fuses.||||reg||1
Z||000006||CUTTER||04-21-2000||12:16 PM||||which one is the fuse for the fan? all the lettering is rubed off of my fuse box. And where is the relay? what is the relay?||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||04-21-2000||02:32 PM||||Get yourself a Chiltons or a Haynes manual.  They both have nice diagrams.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||04-21-2000||05:28 PM||||I would check the thermostat first.....it's the easiest to check and really cheap to replace.  Another possibility is the fan switch isn't keeping an accurate temp and not telling the fan to turn itself on.<P>To test the fan start the car and turn on the AC.  The fan should kick on.  If it does, then the fan is good and it is likely something else.<P>If you do replace the fan switch I would recommend the Hypertech one.  It turns on the fan at 176*, so your Fiero will run cooler.....the fan will kick on more frequently, but that isn't a biggie.<P>The Fiero Store sells these fan switches for about $45 or you may find it somewhere else cheaper.<BR> <BR>Good luck.   <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 04-21-2000).]||||reg||3
